<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dhimitër Progoni</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1215">1215</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Demetrius Progonos</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dhimitër Progoni was the ruler of Arbanon, the first recorded Albanian principality, from about 1208 to 1215, succeeding his brother Gjin. Styled panhypersebastos, he married Komnena, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-the-first-crowned">Stefan the First-Crowned</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, and corresponded with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-innocent-iii">Pope Innocent III</a> about joining the Catholic Church. After his death Arbanon passed to Gregory Kamonas.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dhimiter-progoni.jpg" alt="Dhimitër Progoni" width="320" height="514" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: unknown · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:DhimiterArbanon.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Dhimitër was a son of Progon of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kruje">Kruja</a>, the archon who held Krujë and its surroundings from at least the 1190s, and he followed his brother Gjin Progoni in power in 1207–08. Before 1204 Arbanon had been an autonomous principality of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>. The empire's collapse in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a> removed Byzantine overlordship, but the agreements dividing the empire awarded Albania to the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, which became his chief opponent, together with the Despotate of Epiros in his later years.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>To defeat Venetian claims he considered leaving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodoxy">Eastern Orthodoxy</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> in 1208–09, since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> could not claim land held by a Catholic ruler. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-innocent-iii">Pope Innocent III</a> instructed Nicolaus, the Catholic archdeacon of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durres">Durrës</a>, to prepare a mission, but Progoni dropped the matter after defeating Đorđe Nemanjić, Grand Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeta-region">Zeta</a> and a Venetian vassal. His wife Komnena was a granddaughter of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexios-iii-angelos">Alexios III Angelos</a>, which brought him the title panhypersebastos. He was excommunicated for seizing church property after the death of the Catholic archbishop of Durrës.</p><p>He granted merchants of the Republic of Ragusa free passage through his lands. In 1210 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> agreed to accept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-i-komnenos-doukas">Michael I Komnenos Doukas</a> of Epiros as its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>, and in 1212 it let the coastal duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durres">Durrës</a> pass to him. Surrounded by hostile powers, Arbanon seems to have shrunk by the end of his life to the land between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shkumbin">Shkumbin</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drin">Drin</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> inscription in the church of Gëziq at Ndërfandë in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mirdita">Mirdita</a>, made after his death, shows that he had been received back into the Catholic Church and had paid for the church, dedicated to Shën Premte. It also served as his will: having no sons, he named his nephew Progon, son of Gjin, as protosevastos and successor. His widow was soon married to Gregory Kamonas. Historians have suggested that the Dukagjini family, recorded in the same region some 70 years later, were related to or descended from the Progoni. Later rulers presented their realms as heirs of his principality, beginning with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-i-of-anjou">Charles I of Anjou</a>'s Kingdom of Albania in 1272.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Demetrio Progoni” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its first known ruler was the archon Progon, around 1190, followed by his sons Gjin and <b>Dhimitër</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-arbanon">Principality of Arbanon</a></li></ul></section>
